## Langfuse scopes project keys for ingestion, feedback, and reporting

Source: Langfuse — https://langfuse.com/changelog/2026-10-16-project-api-key-permissions
Published: 2026-10-16
Channel: news

Langfuse now lets users choose permissions when creating a project API key. Its dated announcement separates four access levels: Full access, Viewer, Ingestion, and Scores ingestion. An Ingestion key can create traces, scores, and media without reading traces, prompts, or datasets. Scores ingestion is limited to submitting scores, while Viewer provides read-only project access for reporting. The configuration lives in Project Settings under API Keys. Existing keys retain their current access, so the feature does not automatically narrow credentials already deployed in applications.

For teams collecting sensitive agent conversations, this provides a practical way to reduce the data exposed by a leaked telemetry credential. Our editorial recommendation is to inventory integrations by the operations they actually perform, then create separate keys for application telemetry, user feedback, and reporting. Verify both permitted and denied operations with each replacement before rotating it into production. Treat feedback submission as a separate trust boundary: a score-only key limits access but does not prove that submitted feedback is authentic. Keep credential scope, expiration, and application-side validation as distinct controls.

Why it matters: Apply the narrowest scope to each integration and explicitly rotate old keys; creating new permission levels does not restrict existing credentials.

